1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
 - Welcome to Networked Programs.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,080
 This is Chapter 12.

3
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,280
 Now we're going to learn a little bit

4
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:10,280
 about how we talk to resources on the network using Python.

5
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:13,680
 Now, this is a really quick introduction

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:14,960
 to how the network really works.

7
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:17,240
 I have a whole book that I wrote.

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,360
 It's also translated into Spanish

9
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:20,840
 on how the network works,

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:23,040
 starting at the very lowest layer,

11
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:25,240
 packets and everything, right on up.

12
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:26,720
 And it's actually really easy to read.

13
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,200
 I wrote it for a high school audience.

14
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,560
 It's a short book and pretty easy to read.

15
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:33,560
 So if you read that book,

16
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:37,600
 you will understand that there is this layered architecture,

17
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,400
 the TCP architecture that sort of runs our network.

18
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,440
 At the lowest layer, on one side here,

19
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:45,400
 this is your computer and this is a server computer.

20
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,520
 And if you sort of want a webpage,

21
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:48,840
 it goes across the network,

22
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,080
 does this like 15 or 20 times,

23
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:54,080
 then it goes up into the server, reads the data,

24
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:58,840
 and then the data comes back, 15, 20 hops for the packets,

25
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:02,120
 and then it's shown to you as what you see.

26
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:06,040
 And so, that's how it works.

27
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:07,400
 And there's these layers

28
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,200
 that we're not going to talk about in this section,

29
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:10,840
 but I talk about in that book.

30
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,080
 The layers of the link layer,

31
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,920
 which talk about how to get over one hop.

32
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:17,920
 The internet layer, which talks about how to construct,

33
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:22,920
 say, 15 or so hops to get packets back and forth.

34
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:26,160
 That's the sort of lower level bits.

35
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,240
 We're going to start at what we call the transport layer,

36
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:31,680
 and that's the layer where your computer sort of assumes

37
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:34,480
 that it can make a phone call to another computer,

38
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,440
 another process running on a program on this computer,

39
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:39,320
 talks to a program on this computer,

40
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:41,480
 and then it kind of comes back, okay?

41
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:44,200
 And so, we're going to leave this alone.

42
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,440
 We're going to ignore it.

43
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,720
 We're going to assume that there's this nice, reliable pipe

44
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,160
 that's going from point A to point B,

45
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:51,480
 and what are we going to do with the pipe?

46
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,280
 But if you're interested, take a look at the book.

47
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,560
 So, we're just going to start with a pipe,

48
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:57,780
 some kind of a connection.

49
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:01,960
 We have two processes, process, process,

50
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,040
 and we have some kind of a connection between them,

51
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:07,640
 and it is a connection that we can both use

52
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:10,760
 to talk and to listen.

53
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:13,480
 In nerd terms, we call these things sockets,

54
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:16,000
 and that is one process running on one computer,

55
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,640
 another process running on one computer,

56
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,280
 another second computer connected through the internet

57
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:24,420
 somehow, and one computer speaks into that socket,

58
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:27,480
 and it comes out, and the other computer returns something,

59
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:30,800
 and it comes, and so this is a bidirectional protocol

60
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,760
 of data, which is a series of, in effect,

61
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,320
 data phone calls between applications.

62
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,800
 So, the application might be, on your side,

63
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,280
 this might be your browser.

64
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,680
 Chrome, Firefox, Internet Explorer.

65
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,480
 On the other side, this is a web server.

66
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:50,440
 Might be Internet IIS, Internet something-something

67
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:54,420
 from Microsoft, or Apache, or Java Tomcat.

68
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:57,520
 There's another program, and you are making phone calls

69
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:58,720
 between these programs.

70
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,840
 Now, in general, these servers here stay up all the time,

71
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,000
 and you sort of just can make a request

72
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,360
 when you feel like it in your program,

73
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:08,640
 but that's what we're going to do,

74
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:11,040
 and this is what we call a socket.

75
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,200
 So, that little connection, that phone call,

76
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,040
 that data phone call, is what we call a socket.

77
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:20,520
 Now, you have to decide which of the systems

78
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:22,880
 you're going to talk to, and then which of the services

79
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,200
 on those systems, or which process.

80
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,000
 And so, we have this concept called port numbers,

81
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,440
 and they're best thought of like extensions on phones.

82
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,880
 So, one organization has one phone number,

83
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:34,480
 and it says, please enter the extension

84
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,420
 of the party you'd like to talk to.

85
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:38,040
 Well, that's kind of what ports are.

86
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:40,160
 They're like, here is, I'm a server,

87
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:41,300
 and I'm connected to the internet.

88
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,580
 Please enter the extension of the process

89
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,500
 that you would like to talk to.

90
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:48,280
 And so, for example, there might be processes

91
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:52,280
 running on various computers, and so the email's known

92
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:55,640
 to hang out on port 25, or extension 25.

93
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:59,080
 Login, insecure login lives on port 23.

94
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:02,680
 Insecure web lives on 80, insecure web lives on 443,

95
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,520
 and there's a couple of different protocols.

96
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:07,040
 Say, if you have your mail stored on Gmail,

97
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:10,240
 and you have a local mail client, say like Thunderbird,

98
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,800
 or Apple Mail, that talks a protocol

99
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,760
 to pull that mail across, and those live on various ports.

100
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,720
 So, these ports are those extensions,

101
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:23,200
 and by convention, we have standards that tell us

102
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:26,040
 what to roughly expect at those ports.

103
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,260
 So, when you're talking to port 80,

104
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:33,000
 you expect to talk to a web server, or an HTTP server.

105
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,080
 If you're talking on port 23, you expect to talk

106
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,880
 to a telnet server, and on and on and on and on.

107
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:40,640
 And so, these are the extensions, the typical,

108
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,480
 commonly used default extensions

109
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:46,500
 for various network application processes

110
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:48,560
 that are serving us data.

111
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:50,240
 Now, sometimes you'll go to a URL,

112
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:53,200
 and you'll see in that URL, there's a colon and a number.

113
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:56,560
 That means it's a web server that's running on a port

114
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:59,600
 other than the official 80 or 443 port.

115
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:04,680
 Now, in Python, we can talk to these sockets, right?

116
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,380
 We can just talk to them, and it's really easy,

117
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:08,960
 surprisingly easy.

118
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:12,480
 We have to import socket, because that's a library.

119
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,200
 It comes with Python, but until you can use it,

120
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,560
 you can't use it in your program until you say it.

121
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,600
 And then, basically, in the socket library,

122
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:26,160
 you call socket function, that's what that syntax is saying.

123
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:28,400
 You're making a socket, now the key to a socket,

124
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,920
 it's sort of like an unopened file handle.

125
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:33,200
 It's half of a file handle.

126
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,400
 It's an outward-looking thing that's not yet connected.

127
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,720
 These parameters, you're just going to type 'em in.

128
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:40,160
 This says we're going to make a socket

129
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:42,600
 that goes across the internet, and it's a stream socket,

130
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,760
 which means that it's a series of characters

131
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,280
 that come one after another,

132
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:48,140
 rather than a series of blocks of text.

133
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:50,340
 There's another kind that's harder to deal with,

134
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:51,400
 but we're going to do this.

135
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,640
 So this, don't worry about this line.

136
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:55,440
 Just know that this creates a socket,

137
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:57,600
 but does not associate it.

138
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:01,560
 The very next line, we get back a socket object

139
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,600
 that I'm storing in the variable mysock,

140
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:05,520
 and then when you want to make a connection

141
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:08,080
 across the internet to the far end,

142
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,160
 you say, oh, hey, dear socket,

143
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,960
 extend yourself across the internet.

144
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:16,740
 Make the phone call to this host, data.pr4e.org,

145
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:18,280
 and on that port 80.

146
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:19,720
 So that's making the phone call.

147
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:21,120
 This is like the phone number,

148
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:22,840
 and this is like the phone extension.

149
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,900
 So that's, we haven't sent any data yet.

150
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:28,700
 We have simply rung the phone of a process,

151
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:30,340
 hopefully living on port 80.

152
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:31,600
 If it's there, great.

153
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:32,640
 This might blow up.

154
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:33,760
 This one here won't blow up,

155
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:35,760
 but this line here could blow up.

156
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:37,360
 If there's nothing sitting on that process,

157
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:39,640
 it would come back and say, oh, you try to call,

158
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:40,520
 you got no answer.

159
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:42,140
 That's a legitimate thing to happen.

160
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:43,980
 Maybe you don't have a network connection,

161
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:46,560
 or maybe that service is down on that server,

162
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:47,960
 or the whole server's down.

163
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:53,360
 So I just, it's kind of amazing

164
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:55,140
 that we're sitting here in Python,

165
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:59,040
 and in three lines, we have probably

166
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:01,200
 a half a million engineers have built this thing

167
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:02,820
 called the internet, all these protocols,

168
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:04,160
 and all this software,

169
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:08,000
 and we just made use of it in three lines of Python.

170
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,960
 In a case, this is one of the reasons

171
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:12,440
 that people absolutely love Python,

172
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,940
 absolutely love Python.

173
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:16,660
 So now that we have a socket,

174
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:19,160
 we have to ask ourselves what kind of data

175
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:20,400
 are we going to send,

176
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:22,600
 and then what kind of data are we going to expect

177
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:24,820
 to receive across that socket?

178
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:28,920
 (gentle music)

179
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:42,540
 (gentle music)

